<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Freiberg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Freiberg is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Ore Mountains</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> was discovered there around 1168, setting off a mining rush that made the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> rich and the town one of the largest in the region. Its mining academy, the Bergakademie, founded in 1765, is the oldest university of mining and metallurgy in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/freiberg.jpg" alt="Freiberg" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kolossos · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Freiberg-Petri-Obermarkt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Freiberg lies on the northern slope of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Ore Mountains</a>, on the Freiberger Mulde, a tributary of the Mulde. Its centre is about 412 m above sea level, about 31 km west-southwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>. The nucleus of the town was the forest village of Christiansdorf in the valley of the Münzbach stream. The unwalled town grew on the two slopes of the valley and on the ridge to the west, so that the streets running to the east of the main axis are very steep. The east is the Lower Town, with its lower market, and the west the Upper Town, with the upper market.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was founded around 1168, after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> discovery led to the first Berggeschrey, or mining rush, and for centuries it was a centre of the mining industry in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Ore Mountains</a>. It belonged to the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> from 1186, to the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> from 1423 and to the Kingdom of Saxony from 1806. A fire destroyed the town in 1484, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> quarter was built afterwards. The Bergakademie, the mining academy, was established in 1765 by Prince Franz Xaver, regent of Saxony, on plans by Friedrich Wilhelm von Oppel and Friedrich Anton von Heynitz.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> mining was discontinued in 1913 because the price of silver had fallen. After it resumed before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, mining of lead, zinc and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> continued until 1969, and the town was dominated by mining and smelting for around 800 years. In 1944 a subcamp of the Flossenbürg concentration camp was built outside the town, housing over 500 women, including survivors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>, until its evacuation in April 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Freiberg has around 41,000 inhabitants. Its historic centre is under monument protection, and together with mining monuments such as the Reiche Zeche ore mine it has been part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Erzgebirge</a>/Krušnohoří Mining Region since 2019. Freiberg Cathedral holds two organs by Gottfried Silbermann, a tulip pulpit and the Golden Gate, and its choir became a burial place of the Albertine Wettins. The university, the oldest of mining and metallurgy in the world, has since 1969 turned the town towards semiconductor and solar technology. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> market keeps the mining traditions of the Ore Mountains.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Freiberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its wealth began with the silver of the Erzgebirge, mined at <b>Freiberg</b> since the twelfth century and, after new strikes from the 1470s, at Schneeberg, Annaberg and Marienberg; the large silver coins struck there from about 1500 were among the first of the type later called the thaler.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The discovery of silver at <b>Freiberg</b> around 1168 made the margraves wealthy; Otto the Rich used it to fortify his lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-meissen">Margraviate of Meissen</a></li></ul></section>
